#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81dea8fe-5a5b-5a29-9aaa-2c0d4f1764e9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cf2092b-4dd8-5577-b36a-cd5c90777daa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570225} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc84bcf-3d54-5493-bbf5-f15198a48f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570263} {:answer/id #uuid "6ccbd482-6f82-5365-be1b-29c7904b3202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570313} {:answer/id #uuid "ba49c28a-0d1a-51dd-9f2d-4aaa2b5101b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570453} {:answer/id #uuid "ff533885-694c-5d8f-b455-8987426b0493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ccbd482-6f82-5365-be1b-29c7904b3202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570313} :answer-field/id #uuid "920aa12b-f974-5b8f-8512-f6303ba5b5ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "25bfd26a-4014-54ec-bb25-41bebf6a0072" :question/math-academy-id "q-144847" :question/problem "![](images/86/861e191e0f0b0d993e2d16f29da17ec9e258d657f6bdfc471e5fa9cf21a67b21.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the maximum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,2]$ given that $f(2)=4.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $1$ trapezoid and $1$ triangle. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/b7/b7f6e2f43ae474ec3194b2395144e8ea7dc9964da65bd018af08bb24b873bdd4.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =4 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-5 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(-3) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-3}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-(-1+5) \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{4,-1,0\\}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "373ecc37-7913-5b59-adea-7ad3d5d78f28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23045462-0c63-5e55-a547-f6d9c1423dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570188} {:answer/id #uuid "4271de1f-2b94-5b54-9703-cefae48d132f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570237} {:answer/id #uuid "6e3a2d1e-d9c5-580c-ac02-4274a8f8aea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570319} {:answer/id #uuid "97cf76f8-44e3-5bf4-98db-081710e81c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570392} {:answer/id #uuid "f3763ea8-131b-51ba-8034-87fce394cdde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23045462-0c63-5e55-a547-f6d9c1423dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570188} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aff9ed3-8040-515b-8916-8efa69b012c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5fcb87aa-38a0-5213-a001-cd7978756f39" :question/math-academy-id "q-144875" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cba90791c239c986390a8a7fe1cb3ea0b4abb9cfd86ca13cba9ece6a5b4860b6.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-5,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the minimum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-5,4]$ given that $f(4)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-5,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-5,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,0,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $1$ trapezoid and $2$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/a8/a8bff8783c0e536cd0e3c475a8fca87a9861a1e1f0ca7f2833ca9c434da2426d.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =1 \\\\ f(0) & =f(4)+{\\int }_{4}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(4)-{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1-(-8) \\\\  & =1+8 \\\\  & =9 \\\\ f(-5) & =f(4)+{\\int }_{4}^{-5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(4)-{\\int }_{-5}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1-(9+2-8) \\\\  & =1-3 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{1,9,-2\\}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "373ecc37-7913-5b59-adea-7ad3d5d78f28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "964ed659-a234-5d78-b3eb-254df1986447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "964ed659-a234-5d78-b3eb-254df1986447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570385} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fef5802-c8af-5407-b40d-c7abf62f6135" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df0b416e-f70a-5924-a6c6-f62bc6887f2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-344165" :question/problem "![](images/73/731237ee8d4ea5fdb53c50971bad11dd9f4317f595ae9af95846b17b6d064fce.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-5,3].$ The graph of ${g}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $g(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $7.$\n\nGiven that $g(3)=4,$ what is the minimum value ${g}_{\\text{min}}$ of $g(x)$ on the interval $[-5,3]?$\n\n${g}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $g(x)$ on $[-5,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${g}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-5,3),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $g$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & =4 \\\\ g(-5) & =g(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-5}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =g(3)-{\\int }_{-5}^{3}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-7 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $g$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $g$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{4,-3\\}=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $g$ is ${g}_{\\text{min}}=-3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $g(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-5.$"}]}
